// The layout every ymux pane's zellij session is created with.
//
// Selected BY NAME from ymux-zellij.kdl (`default_layout "ymux"`), and found
// in <ZELLIJ_CONFIG_DIR>/layouts. Verified with `zellij setup --check` on
// 0.44.3 (2026-08-20): setting ZELLIJ_CONFIG_DIR=<dir> makes [LAYOUT DIR]
// resolve to <dir>\layouts, and leaves [DATA DIR] / [PLUGIN DIR] where they
// were under %APPDATA%\Zellij.
//
// That indirection is the whole point. A bundled resource ships the same
// bytes to every machine, so it cannot contain an absolute install path —
// and `zellij attach` has no --layout flag to pass one on the command line.
//
// Zellij's own default, for comparison (`zellij setup --dump-layout default`):
//
//     layout {
//         pane size=1 borderless=true { plugin location="tab-bar" }
//         pane
//         pane size=1 borderless=true { plugin location="status-bar" }
//     }
//
// Two plugin rows, one at each edge. THOSE are what "the frame is still
// there" actually was: `pane_frames false` removes the border AROUND a pane
// and never touched them. This file removes them by not asking for them,
// which gives every ymux pane back two rows.
//
// ONE pane. No tab node (zellij wraps a bare pane list in one tab, exactly as
// the default above does), no plugins, no swap layouts.
//
// Do not add a second pane. ymux owns splitting, and a second zellij pane
// would be chrome the user cannot see, focus or close — and with keybinds
// cleared it could not be escaped either. It also keeps
// `zellij -s <name> action write-chars` (the connect wizard, lib.rs) honest:
// it targets the FOCUSED pane, and there is exactly one.
layout {
    pane
}
